{"date": "2026-10-29", "post_id": 1505211, "title": "The Horns of Venus", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-29-october-2026-the-horns-of-venus/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://tenor.com/view/thats-no-moon-obi-wan-kenobi-starwars-death-star-gif-10170550\">That's no moon.</a> But you could be forgiven for thinking it was a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/moon-phases/\">crescent Moon</a> in the sky. It is a lesser known fact that <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phases_of_Venus\">Venus also has phases</a>. (What about <a href=\"https://www.pas.rochester.edu/~blackman/ast104/aspects6.html\">other planets?</a>) The <a href=\"http://www.astrophoto.fr/20261024_venus_inferior_conjunction.html\">featured image</a> shows <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/venus/\">Venus</a> seen almost directly between the Sun and the Earth. This happens once every <a href=\"https://astro.dur.ac.uk/~ams/users/inner_rest.html\">year and a half</a>, and it is like the alignment that happens every month during a new Moon. But the new Moon is dark, while Venus' backlit <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-july-3-venus-in-ultraviolet-from-akatsuki/\">atmosphere</a> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/03_behaviors/\">refracts and scatters</a> the sunlight, creating a glowing <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-august-26-crescents-of-venus/\">crescent arc</a> that extends beyond 180 degrees: the tips of this arc are called the \"horns\". The crescent can be seen with a small telescope or binoculars during twilight, but those with <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1497119146420-012f8fc80a3a\">exceptionally acute eyesight</a> may be able to see it with the unaided eye. Ancient <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venus_tablet_of_Ammisaduqa\">Babylonian</a> astronomers may have been the first to study Venus' brightness changes, which are related to its <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-january-8-the-phases-of-venus/\">phases</a>. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venus_in_culture\">Many other cultures</a> have similar astronomical records, including the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dresden_Codex\">oldest </a><a href=\"https://www.loc.gov/resource/gdcwdl.wdl_11621/?st=gallery\">surviving</a><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dresden_Codex\"> Maya book</a>. <br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> What's next?", "credit": "<a href=\"http://www.astrophoto.fr/\">Thierry Legault</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"http://www.astrophoto.fr/\">Thierry Legault</a>", "alt": "See Explanation.  Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-29-october-2026-the-horns-of-venus/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/venus_20261024a.jpg?w=744&h=744&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 29 - The Horns of Venus</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-29-october-2026-the-horns-of-venus/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Cecilia Chirenti\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: That&#039;s no moon. But you could be forgiven for thinking it was a crescent Moon in the sky. It is a lesser known fact that Venus also has phases. (What about other planets?) The featured image shows Venus seen almost directly between the Sun and the Earth. This happens once every year and a half, and it is like the alignment that happens every month during a new Moon. But the new Moon is dark, while Venus&#039; backlit atmosphere refracts and scatters the sunlight, creating a glowing crescent arc that extends beyond 180 degrees: the tips of this arc are called the &quot;horns&quot;. The crescent can be seen with a small telescope or binoculars during twilight, but those with exceptionally acute eyesight may be able to see it with the unaided eye. Ancient Babylonian astronomers may have been the first to study Venus&#039; brightness changes, which are related to its phases. Many other cultures have similar astronomical records, including the oldest surviving Maya book. Tomorrow&#039;s picture: What&#039;s next?\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"the horns of venus\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"The Horns of Venus\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-29-october-2026-the-horns-of-venus/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/venus_20261024a.jpg?w=744&#038;h=744&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/venus_20261024a.jpg?w=744&#038;h=744&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"See Explanation.  Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-29T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-28T14:29:26-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"The Horns of Venus\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/venus_20261024a.jpg?w=744&#038;h=744&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 29<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/venus_20261024a.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/venus_20261024a.jpg\" alt=\"See Explanation.  Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> The Horns of Venus </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"http://www.astrophoto.fr/\">Thierry Legault</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://tenor.com/view/thats-no-moon-obi-wan-kenobi-starwars-death-star-gif-10170550\">That's no moon.</a> But you could be forgiven for thinking it was a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/moon-phases/\">crescent Moon</a> in the sky. It is a lesser known fact that <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phases_of_Venus\">Venus also has phases</a>. (What about <a href=\"https://www.pas.rochester.edu/~blackman/ast104/aspects6.html\">other planets?</a>) The <a href=\"http://www.astrophoto.fr/20261024_venus_inferior_conjunction.html\">featured image</a> shows <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/venus/\">Venus</a> seen almost directly between the Sun and the Earth. This happens once every <a href=\"https://astro.dur.ac.uk/~ams/users/inner_rest.html\">year and a half</a>, and it is like the alignment that happens every month during a new Moon. But the new Moon is dark, while Venus' backlit <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-july-3-venus-in-ultraviolet-from-akatsuki/\">atmosphere</a> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/03_behaviors/\">refracts and scatters</a> the sunlight, creating a glowing <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-august-26-crescents-of-venus/\">crescent arc</a> that extends beyond 180 degrees: the tips of this arc are called the \"horns\". The crescent can be seen with a small telescope or binoculars during twilight, but those with <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1497119146420-012f8fc80a3a\">exceptionally acute eyesight</a> may be able to see it with the unaided eye. Ancient <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venus_tablet_of_Ammisaduqa\">Babylonian</a> astronomers may have been the first to study Venus' brightness changes, which are related to its <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-january-8-the-phases-of-venus/\">phases</a>. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venus_in_culture\">Many other cultures</a> have similar astronomical records, including the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dresden_Codex\">oldest </a><a href=\"https://www.loc.gov/resource/gdcwdl.wdl_11621/?st=gallery\">surviving</a><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dresden_Codex\"> Maya book</a>. <br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> What's next?\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-28-the-mutual-occultation-of-ganymede-and-io/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nCecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261029/html"}